logy  neuroscience  and many other disciplines as well we  will also provide a link to the select  science website where you can access  independent reviews and customer  testimonials for all of the live cell  imaging technologies discussed here  today  okay so at this point i'd like to open  the floor to a q a session i can see a  number of questions are already coming  in from the audience here  uh so please continue to provide your  questions for down on the team by the  question box  uh in the webinar toolbar and we'll  continue to address them for you  so looking at what we've got here at the  moment um we've got a question from  carter uh so this  is a question about the t-cell killing  essay so why start at 20  confluence for short-term assay like  adcc  wouldn't you want more targeted cells so  that you have a denser image  especially if you are anticipating the  lost type of cells by affected cell  killing so  i guess this is a combination of  proliferation so there  yeah that's a that's a great question  and  it's perfectly reasonable standpoint we  have  taken the view of building our uh assays  to grow cells from lower confluence and  look for  effects on proliferation as well as the  appearance of dead cells but  absolutely you could equally set these  cells up  at a much higher confluence and look for  a reduction  in cell number and an appearance of  apoptotic cells  the only downside i can think of that  approach is  if the killing time  course is slow then potentially you will  overwhelm the cell cultures when when  they reach confluence before  you see any killing but i i have to  agree with the  the suggestion that potentially you  could do  a particularly for adcc where we know  that the killing profile  can be relatively uh relatively quick  but you could certainly um and and in  this case also the skull  iii cells are not so precious in as much  as that they're an immortalized cell  line  you could certainly use a higher cell  number to begin with so yeah that's a  great comment thank you  okay i've got a question here from susan  i like your example of neuronal  different differentiation  can you provide some other examples of  the use of lifestyle analysis to monitor  cell manipulations  okay thank you susan yeah that's that's  a great question um  i just uh picked two examples for today  but yes there are  certainly others off the top of my head  a couple that i can think of  we recently did a study with human  monocytes where we took monocytes and we  use differentiation  or two different differentiation  protocols to  induce the macrophy macrophage phenotype  so we  differentiated cells into m1 and m2  macrophages where we could verify that  the  difference in in cell morphology using  continuous lifestyle analysis  and then we use those self-same cells to  do  um real-time chemotaxis assays so that  that was  i think a nice example where life cell  analysis was used through the cell  husbandry  through to the the kinetic assay  another slightly different uh example i  can think of  is uh clonal dilution where  we did some experiments to  try to generate truly clonal cell lines  so  we seeded them out at very low density  on plates so typically less than  half a cell per well and then look for  the emergence of  colonies over time so  that was a a nice way again where  you could use the ability to review  historic data  and couple them to  later time points to really wind back to  see when  healthy colonies emerge that they really  truly form from a single  cell so hopefully those two examples are  helpful thank you thank you  a question here from kylie  could we take your adcc protocol for  ic50 calculation  yes yes we could yes i somewhat glossed  over the  the pharmacology that that came out at  the end of that but we have routinely  been  using that uh protocol to test  herceptin biosimilars for example we've  similar assay  in suspension cells where we've been  looking at rituximab and ritoximab